WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.644
This is one of my favorite&nbsp;
Spider-Man scenes of all time.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.920
I love the way the suit ripples in the wind

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.400
I love the crazy angles, I love the music

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.600
and how Andrew Garfield’s Spider-Man&nbsp;
waits until the absolute last second

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.777
before shooting a web.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.520
It’s the epitome of Spider-Man&nbsp;.

00:00:20.347 --> 00:00:23.833
But of course, we’re not really&nbsp;
seeing Andrew Garfield here.

00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:27.120
this is a digidouble: a fully&nbsp;
digital version of the character.

00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:30.442
In fact, this whole scene is digital.

00:00:30.442 --> 00:00:31.547
Obviously, right?

00:00:31.547 --> 00:00:33.166
A real person couldn’t do this.

00:00:33.787 --> 00:00:37.836
Digital doubles aren’t just used for&nbsp;
impossible action sequences, though.

00:00:37.836 --> 00:00:40.000
Take these three clips of Spider-Man.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.243
First is from the original Spider-Man from 2002.

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:49.200
Second is Spidey’s first appearance in the&nbsp;
MCU in 2016’s "Captain America: Civil War".

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.797
Third is Spidey surrounded by drones in the&nbsp;
Illusion sequence in 2019s "Far From Home".

00:00:54.797 --> 00:00:58.313
Can you guess which on is filmed with&nbsp;
an actor and which one is a digidouble?

00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:01.657
Okay, it’s kind of a trick question.

00:01:02.156 --> 00:01:03.680
None of these are real people.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.612
They’re all digidoubles.

00:01:05.612 --> 00:01:09.372
In fact, a ton of superheroes&nbsp;
are just digital replacements

00:01:09.372 --> 00:01:11.737
even in the most mundane scenes.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:13.063
But why?

00:01:14.922 --> 00:01:19.216
Superhero movies and digidoubles&nbsp;
have always paired well together.

00:01:19.216 --> 00:01:21.577
And not just because of the stunts.

00:01:21.577 --> 00:01:25.840
It’s because of the costumes and the masks.

00:01:26.747 --> 00:01:30.400
Now might be the time to admit that&nbsp;
I’m a little bit of a Spider-Man fan.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.360
Anyway, replicating a fabric or metallic costume

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.688
is way easier than replicating&nbsp;
skin or a human face.

00:01:43.688 --> 00:01:46.876
Which gives the effects artists a lot of leeway.

00:01:46.876 --> 00:01:50.107
They can mix real and digital&nbsp;
elements for maximum realism.

00:01:50.107 --> 00:01:54.240
Like keeping a bit of an actor's face,&nbsp;
and replacing everything else digitally

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.396
like in this scene from Spider-Man.

00:01:56.396 --> 00:01:59.920
Or maybe they just replace everything&nbsp;
from the neck down, like with Iron Man

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.441
and then they add a digidouble&nbsp;
of War Machine next to him.

00:02:03.441 --> 00:02:05.840
Or maybe they’ll just replace everything from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.880
ankle down to create toes&nbsp;
worthy of a super soldier.

00:02:12.172 --> 00:02:16.960
Okay, that one is a little less&nbsp;
common, but more often than not

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.337
they’ll cut from a real actor on film

00:02:19.337 --> 00:02:22.640
to a digital recreation without too much friction.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.400
And while some of these digital&nbsp;replacements 
are planned from the beginning

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.332
sometimes they're not.

00:02:28.332 --> 00:02:32.187
Which is what happened with Spider-Man’s&nbsp;
first appearance in the MCU.

00:02:32.187 --> 00:02:35.000
He was digidouble in every shot.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.628
Both when he was swinging around Giantman's legs

00:02:37.628 --> 00:02:41.113
and when he was just standing there, talking.

00:02:41.113 --> 00:02:44.560
That’s because he almost wasn’t&nbsp;
in "Captain America: Civil War"

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.600
due to lengthy negotiations&nbsp;
between Sony and Marvel

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.756
about how to share the web-slinger.

00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:53.280
Tom Holland wasn’t even cast until June of 2015

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.120
a month after filming began.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.200
So once he was confirmed, there was&nbsp;
a huge rush to get him in the movie

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.840
as Holland explains on Live with Kelly and Ryan

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.324
“The suits typically take&nbsp;10 and 15 weeks 
to make, right?

00:03:04.324 --> 00:03:06.480
And they didn’t have enough&nbsp;
time to make one for me

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.626
so I was going to wear my stunt doubles one.“

00:03:08.626 --> 00:03:09.920
What he ended up wearing on set

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.640
which you can see in this&nbsp;
behind the scenes footage

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.528
was not finalized by the costume department.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.924
Which is where the digidouble comes in.

00:03:17.924 --> 00:03:21.612
VFX artists changed his entire costume in post.

00:03:21.612 --> 00:03:23.947
None of the final version is real fabric.

00:03:23.947 --> 00:03:26.667
And it’s different from the suit worn on set too.

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:29.817
You can see that the spider&nbsp;
symbol on the chest is refined

00:03:29.817 --> 00:03:34.408
and the webbing across the body isn’t&nbsp;
raised like previous renditions of the suit.

00:03:34.408 --> 00:03:36.942
It’s stitched in, and more subtle.

00:03:36.942 --> 00:03:42.202
Holland did some motion capture for his scenes&nbsp;
but that was just the base of the digital double.

00:03:42.202 --> 00:03:46.236
Animators had to clean it up and push&nbsp;
it further with the new digital suit.

00:03:46.236 --> 00:03:50.240
Animators like to kind of take things&nbsp;
and they like to exaggerate movement.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.040
And sometimes it's what feels right.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.640
You know, not what is right&nbsp;
especially for superheroes.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.640
You know, you may want exaggerated movement 
that a human cannot do.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:06.488
Lon Molnar is the Co-President of the VFX&nbsp;
company Monsters Aliens Robots and Zombies.

00:04:06.488 --> 00:04:10.073
They worked on full CG versions&nbsp;
of Vision and Moonknight

00:04:10.073 --> 00:04:12.980
as well as de-aging for the&nbsp;
villains in Spider-Man: No Way Home.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.160
"Hello, Peter."

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.520
Even when motion capture is involved, VFX artist&nbsp;
still consider characters to be digidoubles.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.547
In our sense, in our world,&nbsp;
it is a digidouble still.

00:04:23.547 --> 00:04:26.469
Because you're recreating everything digitally.

00:04:26.469 --> 00:04:31.040
You're relighting the suit, you're re-composing,&nbsp;
you're erasing the main performance.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.760
Everything in the shot is taken over by us.

00:04:34.747 --> 00:04:36.831
Costume issues come up all the time.

00:04:37.673 --> 00:04:42.876
The timesuits from "Avengers: Endgame" weren’t&nbsp;
finalized until after everything was shot.

00:04:42.876 --> 00:04:45.311
Neither was the integrated suit from No Way Home.

00:04:45.932 --> 00:04:49.840
That's all iterating, iterating.
Marvel loves to iterate.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.120
And tweaks after the fact, just make&nbsp;
it look more superhero-ish, right?

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.336
And looking superhero-ish is another&nbsp;
big reason for digidoubles.

00:05:00.336 --> 00:05:04.640
As an animator myself, one of the&nbsp;
things traditionally that you learn

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.040
is how strong posing is really&nbsp;
important that silhouette.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.840
In this scene from Civil War where Black&nbsp;
Panther is also always a digidouble

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.360
you can see how, in the final&nbsp;
version, the hands are moved out

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.863
so that they don’t blend into the body,
and the body position is more upright.

00:05:22.543 --> 00:05:27.280
Digidoubles are even used to make superheroes&nbsp;
look more like comic book superheroes

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.240
as the VFX team on the first Iron Man describe

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.353
“We try to get what we’ve&nbsp;
been calling 'a Marvel moment'

00:05:33.353 --> 00:05:38.774
which is sort of, you know, here he’s really&nbsp;
flying through the air and he’s got his fist up."

00:05:38.774 --> 00:05:42.160
They're trying to match maybe a comic&nbsp;
book image, so the fans just [indistinct].

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.960
Their heads explode and "that's&nbsp;
that shot!" And you know.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.880
They want it to be perfect.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.876
And with a full CG replacement they can hit that.

00:05:53.251 --> 00:05:58.857
Integrating digidoubles seamlessly can convince&nbsp;
audiences that a regular human is super.

00:05:58.857 --> 00:06:03.760
That means that these “live action” films are&nbsp;often
way more animated than we give them credit for.

00:06:04.598 --> 00:06:06.890
According to the DVD extras on the film

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:12.862
96%&nbsp;&nbsp;of all the shots making up 
"Avengers:&nbsp;Infinity War" had some kind of VFX.

00:06:12.862 --> 00:06:14.800
That’s fairly common for Marvel.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:21.513
All of their movies have thousands and thousands&nbsp;
of VFX shots, and those often include digidoubles.

00:06:21.513 --> 00:06:26.808
This gives filmmakers the space and flexibility&nbsp;
to get things exactly how they want them.

00:06:26.808 --> 00:06:29.600
With Marvel, they're making&nbsp;
it a tool in the toolbox

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.760
to allow them to get over some of these challenges

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.120
because they know that VFX companies&nbsp;
can make things look so real.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.327
So, it's a great tool for them&nbsp;
to have full confidence in.

